	'admissions_s1_h' => 'The four stages',
	'admissions_s1_p1' => 'A cohort always begins in the same four stages, in this order. First is Open Bench: free, on the first Saturday of the month, from 10:00 to 13:00, to try a torch under supervision. Second is the application: a one-page form, and no experience is needed. Third is the bench talk: 30 minutes with Claire Dubois and one instructor. Fourth is day one: a $500 deposit secures a booth, and the kit is issued on the first morning. The kit is a helmet, a jacket, gloves and boots. Skipping a stage is not how a place is given. A conversation at Open Bench is not the bench talk, and a completed form does not by itself secure a booth. The booth is secured when the deposit is paid on day one.',
	'admissions_s2_h' => 'Cohort dates',
	'admissions_s2_p1' => 'Three cohorts start each year, on 11 January, 3 May and 6 September. Each one is the Six-Month Course: 26 weeks, Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30, seven taught hours a day, 728 hours. Twelve students make a cohort. Two instructors are on the floor, at a ratio of 1:6. The application and the bench talk are how the college decides who takes one of those 12 places. Claire Dubois handles admissions and placements, and she is in every bench talk along with one instructor. After the cohort begins, the same office arranges the two-week placement in a local shop during month 6. Someone aiming at a particular start should say so on the form. The office is at 27 Slipway Road, Port Aldren, and answers on weekdays from 9:00 to 17:00.',
	'admissions_s3_h' => 'Who it suits',
	'admissions_s3_p1' => 'The course is for adults changing trade, including people who have never held a torch. Marit Solberg founded the college in 2011 for that student. It suits someone who can be in the workshop Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30, for 26 weeks, and who wants Fridays left free for a part-time job. It does not ask for earlier welding, a portfolio or a particular kind of former work. A dental receptionist, a delivery driver and a line cook are all among the people who have trained here. Anyone who cannot attend through the day can read about Evening Foundations, which runs on Tuesday and Thursday evenings, before assuming the day cohort is the only door. The bench talk is 30 minutes, and it is a conversation, not a trade test.',
	'admissions_s4_h' => 'Open Bench and the line we keep',
	'admissions_s4_p1' => 'Open Bench is the first Saturday of each month, 10:00 to 13:00, and it is free. Visitors try a torch under supervision. It is the right first visit if you are unsure about the fee or the length. After it, the one-page application and the bench talk are the next two stages, and only then day one. To ask about the next first Saturday, or about 11 January, 3 May and 6 September, write to hello@hibanacollege.example, call +1 555 0142, or use the enquiry form on the Contact page. We teach the trade and arrange the placement. We cannot promise a job or a test result. Coming to Open Bench does not oblige you to apply, and applying does not oblige you to pay the deposit until you have had the bench talk and have chosen a cohort.',
