	'graduate_stories_s1_h' => 'Nadia Rahman',
	'graduate_stories_s1_p1' => 'Nadia Rahman was a dental receptionist. She is now a fabricator at Slipway Railings. She trained at Hibana College with no background at a torch, which is the ordinary starting point here: the application asks for no experience. The Six-Month Course she came through is 26 weeks, Monday to Thursday, seven taught hours a day, 728 hours in a cohort of 12. The months run from a squared, cut and filed steel block, through a T-joint that has to pass a break test, a vertical-up fillet plate, a clean stainless butt joint and a welded steel gate frame, to a test plate set seen by a visiting independent inspector. Two weeks of that last month are placement in a local shop. Slipway Railings is where Nadia works now. Her path is one story. It is not an offer of the same job to the next person who applies.',
	'graduate_stories_s2_h' => 'Owen Clarke',
	'graduate_stories_s2_p1' => 'Owen Clarke was a delivery driver. He started the course at 41, and he is now a welder at Aldren Marine Repair. The college was opened for adults changing trade, including people who had never held a torch, and Owen\'s start at 41 sits inside that purpose. He went through the same Six-Month Course as the other graduates on this page: 12 students, two instructors on the floor at 1:6, Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30. Fridays stayed free, which is how many students keep a part-time job during the 26 weeks. Month 6 included test plates seen by a visiting independent inspector and two weeks on placement in a local shop. Aldren Marine Repair is the workshop where he welds now. Starting at 41 is a fact of his story. It is not an age rule, and it is not a promise that the same employer will take the next cohort.',
	'graduate_stories_s3_h' => 'Beatriz Lima',
	'graduate_stories_s3_p1' => 'Beatriz Lima was a line cook. She is now a fitter at Northmole Steelwork. Kitchen work and fitting share long shifts and a respect for order, and they share little else in tools. She learned the trade here, on the course that runs from Bench & Safety through fabrication to test pieces. The fabrication month asks for layout, cutting, fit-up and distortion, and it ends with a welded steel gate frame. The last month asks for a test plate set and an inspector\'s report, plus two weeks on placement in a local shop. Claire Dubois arranges that placement. Northmole Steelwork is where Beatriz works now, as a fitter. The college can describe her change of work. It cannot treat her job as a sample of what the next 12 students will be offered when their own 26 weeks end.',
	'graduate_stories_s4_h' => 'What these stories are not',
	'graduate_stories_s4_p1' => 'We teach the trade and arrange the placement. We cannot promise a job or a test result. Nadia Rahman, Owen Clarke and Beatriz Lima are three graduates. Their work before the course and their work now are stated so a visitor can see real changes of trade, not a rate and not a salary. The college does not publish a pass rate, a typical wage or a list of employers who must take students. Placement in month 6 is two weeks in a local shop. It is arranged. It is not an offer of permanent work, and the inspector\'s report is the visiting inspector\'s document, not a certificate from Hibana College. Read the stories as three accounts. Applications are still a one-page form, and no experience is required.',
