	'instructors_p2_tab' => 'PRIYA NAIR',
	'instructors_p2_word' => 'steel & stainless',
	'instructors_s1_h' => 'Tomas Reyes and Priya Nair',
	'instructors_s1_p1' => 'Tomas Reyes is lead instructor for MIG and stick. Priya Nair is the instructor for TIG. On the Six-Month Course those subjects are taught in order. Month 2 is MIG on Plate: flat and horizontal fillets, and a T-joint that passes a break test. Month 3 is Stick & Position: stick, vertical and overhead, and a vertical-up fillet plate. Month 4 is TIG, Steel & Stainless: torch and filler control, and a clean stainless butt joint. Two instructors are on the floor with every cohort of 12, which is a ratio of 1:6. Tomas leads the MIG and stick work. Priya leads the TIG work. They teach inside the 26 weeks and the day hours, Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30, not as visiting lecturers between other jobs.',
	'instructors_s2_h' => 'Joseph Oduya and Lena Hartmann',
	'instructors_s2_p1' => 'Joseph Oduya is the instructor for fabrication. That work is month 5: layout, cutting, fit-up and distortion, and the piece that leaves the bench is a welded steel gate frame. The month is where plate and position have to become an object that stays in shape. Lena Hartmann teaches drawing and inspection. Reading a drawing begins in month 1, and every workshop week opens on Monday with demonstration and drawing. Inspection is the other part of her teaching. In month 6, test plates are seen by a visiting independent inspector, and the outcome of that visit is the inspector\'s report. Lena teaches students how to read a drawing and how to prepare a plate for that visit. She is not the visiting inspector, and her class is not a substitute for the inspector\'s report.',
	'instructors_s3_h' => 'Samir Haddad, Claire Dubois and Marit Solberg',
	'instructors_s3_p1' => 'Samir Haddad is workshop and safety officer. Month 1 starts with PPE, fume, grinding and cutting, and Samir gives the first-day briefing before a student works alone in a booth. The kit issued that morning is a helmet, a jacket, gloves and boots. Claire Dubois looks after admissions and placements. The bench talk, 30 minutes with Claire and one instructor, is the third stage before a cohort begins. She also arranges the two weeks of placement in a local shop during month 6. Marit Solberg is principal and founder. She left shipyard fitting to teach adults who had never held a torch, and she founded Hibana College in 2011. The workshop and the office are at 27 Slipway Road, Port Aldren. Office hours are Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 17:00.',
	'journal_h1' => 'Journal',
	'journal_intro' => 'The journal is the college\'s own writing: workshop notes, and the three graduate stories published as posts. It does not introduce a second set of facts.',
	'journal_next' => 'Admissions',
	'journal_s1_h' => 'Notes from the workshop',
	'journal_s1_p1' => 'The journal carries writing from Hibana College about how the workshop actually runs. Pieces describe the teaching week, the first Saturday, and the months of the Six-Month Course, using the same hours and the same fees as the rest of the site. A week is still Monday demonstration and drawing, Tuesday and Wednesday in the booth, Thursday morning cut, bend and review, Thursday afternoon clean down and the next drawing. A cohort is still 12 students and two instructors. The journal does not add a different timetable or a different price. It is here so someone considering a change of trade can read the work in sentences before they stand at a bench. Office questions still go to hello@hibanacollege.example or +1 555 0142, Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 17:00.',
	'journal_s2_h' => 'Graduate stories and everything else',
	'journal_s2_p1' => 'The three graduate stories are also posts here: Nadia Rahman, who was a dental receptionist and is now a fabricator at Slipway Railings; Owen Clarke, who was a delivery driver, started at 41, and is now a welder at Aldren Marine Repair; and Beatriz Lima, who was a line cook and is now a fitter at Northmole Steelwork. They sit beside the workshop notes as named accounts, not as a rate. We teach the trade and arrange the placement. We cannot promise a job or a test result. Nothing in the journal is an award, a pass rate or a salary. If a piece mentions the fee, it is $9,800 for the Six-Month Course, $2,400 for Evening Foundations, $980 for the TIG Short Course, $180 for Taster Day, or $45 for one 3-hour Graduate Booth Hire. Open Bench stays free.',
