	'six_month_course_p6_word' => 'Month',
	'six_month_course_s1_h' => 'Twenty-six weeks on the floor',
	'six_month_course_s1_p1' => 'The Six-Month Course is 26 weeks, Monday to Thursday, with seven taught hours a day. That is 728 hours. The day runs from 8:30 to 16:30. Each cohort takes 12 students, with two instructors on the floor, a ratio of 1:6. The workshop has 24 booths. Three cohorts start in a year, on 11 January, 3 May and 6 September. A student stays with one cohort from the first morning through month 6. The work changes every month. The hours, the days and the size of the group do not. Fridays are not used as overflow. If a piece needs more time, it is finished inside the four taught days, not by adding a Friday class.',
	'six_month_course_s2_h' => 'Months 1 to 3',
	'six_month_course_s2_p1' => 'Month 1 is Bench & Safety. Students learn PPE, fume, grinding, cutting and reading a drawing. What leaves the bench is a squared, cut and filed steel block. Month 2 is MIG on Plate. The welds are flat and horizontal fillets, and the piece is a T-joint that passes a break test. Month 3 is Stick & Position. Students work with stick, vertical and overhead, and the piece is a vertical-up fillet plate. The first month is hand tools, fume and the drawing. The second asks a fillet to survive being broken open. The third moves the same standard off the flat bench and into position. Each month keeps one process in front of the cohort until the marked piece is made.',
	'six_month_course_s3_h' => 'Months 4 to 6',
	'six_month_course_s3_p1' => 'Month 4 is TIG, Steel & Stainless. The teaching is torch and filler control, and what leaves the bench is a clean stainless butt joint. Month 5 is Fabrication: layout, cutting, fit-up and distortion, brought together as a welded steel gate frame. Month 6 is Test Pieces & Placement. Test plates are seen by a visiting independent inspector, and students spend two weeks on placement in a local shop. What leaves the bench is a test plate set and an inspector\'s report. The inspector is independent of the college. The report belongs to that visit. Placement is time in a local shop, arranged by the college, for those two weeks and no further claim.',
	'six_month_course_s4_h' => 'Fridays, and the fee',
	'six_month_course_s4_p1' => 'Fridays are free on purpose. Many students keep a part-time job while they train, and the course leaves that day alone so the job can stay. The taught week is Monday to Thursday only. The fee for the Six-Month Course is $9,800, with the kit included. A $500 deposit secures a booth, and the kit is issued on the first morning: helmet, jacket, gloves and boots. The rest is six monthly payments of $1,550, which brings the total to $9,800. Open Bench, before any of this, is free. The full list of fees, the withdrawal rule and the shorter courses are set out on the Fees page.',
	'st1_n' => 'Nadia Rahman',
	'st1_p' => 'Her six months ran from the bench and the drawing to a welded steel frame, then to two weeks on placement. She says the week she learned to read a drawing at speed was the one that changed how the rest felt.',
	'st1_t' => 'Dental receptionist → fabricator at Slipway Railings',
	'st2_n' => 'Owen Clarke',
	'st2_p' => 'He kept his Friday delivery shifts through the course and learned stick overhead in the booth on the other four days. His first plate failed the break test twice before it passed, and he kept both coupons.',
	'st2_t' => 'Delivery driver, started at 41 → welder at Aldren Marine Repair',
	'st3_n' => 'Beatriz Lima',
	'st3_p' => 'She came in through an Open Bench Saturday with no torch experience, worked up to inspector-read test plates in month six, and spent her two placement weeks in the shop where she now works.',
	'st3_t' => 'Line cook → fitter at Northmole Steelwork',
