	'workshop_safety_p2_tab' => 'THE FLOOR',
	'workshop_safety_p2_word' => 'booths',
	'workshop_safety_s1_h' => 'Booths',
	'workshop_safety_s1_p1' => 'The workshop at 27 Slipway Road, Port Aldren, has 24 booths. A Six-Month cohort uses that floor as a group of 12 students, with two instructors present, a ratio of 1:6. Students are not left to share a single bay. Day hours are Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30. Evening Foundations uses Tuesday and Thursday, 18:00 to 21:00. The aisle, the curtains and the booths stay in the same arrangement whoever is in. Graduate Booth Hire, a single 3-hour session at $45, is booked so it does not take a booth away from a cohort in session. The first time a new student is in a booth alone comes after the first-day briefing, not before it. Visitors on Open Bench try a torch under supervision and do not take a booth as their own.',
	'workshop_safety_s2_h' => 'Fume extraction and PPE',
	'workshop_safety_s2_p1' => 'Month 1 is Bench & Safety, and it includes PPE, fume, grinding, cutting and reading a drawing. Fume extraction is part of how the booths are used, taught from the first month rather than added as a note once welding has started. Students learn what the extraction is there for before they spend Tuesday and Wednesday in the booth on their own piece. PPE is taught in that same month, and it matches the kit issued on the first morning of the Six-Month Course: helmet, jacket, gloves and boots. The kit is included in the course fee. Grinding and cutting are practised on the way to the month 1 piece, a squared, cut and filed steel block. Safety in this workshop is that sequence: briefing, protection, extraction, then the tool.',
	'workshop_safety_s3_h' => 'Samir Haddad\'s first-day briefing',
	'workshop_safety_s3_p1' => 'Samir Haddad is the workshop and safety officer. On day one he gives the first-day briefing before a student works alone in a booth. The $500 deposit has secured that booth, and the kit is issued the same morning, so the briefing and the equipment arrive together. Samir covers how the booth is used, how fume extraction is used, and what the helmet, jacket, gloves and boots are for while the cohort is still on Bench & Safety. Later months change the process, from MIG to stick, TIG and fabrication. They do not replace the briefing. Thursday afternoon of every workshop week includes clean down, which is how the booth is left for the next drawing. Questions about the workshop go to the office on weekdays, 9:00 to 17:00.',
	'workshop_week_alt2' => 'A row of finished marked practice pieces on a steel rack',
	'workshop_week_h1' => 'A Workshop Week',
	'workshop_week_intro' => 'Each teaching week on the Six-Month Course runs Monday to Thursday and ends with one marked piece. Friday is left clear on purpose.',
	'workshop_week_next' => 'The Six-Month Course',
	'workshop_week_p1_alt' => 'Student in a booth with a MIG torch on a T-joint',
	'workshop_week_p1_big' => '4',
	'workshop_week_p1_bold' => 'Drawing, booth, review',
	'workshop_week_p1_det' => 'Monday is demonstration and drawing, Tuesday and Wednesday are booth days, Thursday closes the week.',
	'workshop_week_p1_tab' => 'MON–THU',
	'workshop_week_p1_word' => 'taught days',
	'workshop_week_p2_alt' => 'Macro photograph of weld bead ripples across a steel plate',
	'workshop_week_p2_big' => '1',
	'workshop_week_p2_bold' => 'On the review rack',
	'workshop_week_p2_det' => 'Every week ends with one marked piece, looked at close.',
	'workshop_week_p2_tab' => 'THURSDAY',
	'workshop_week_p2_word' => 'marked piece',
	'workshop_week_s1_h' => 'Monday to Thursday',
	'workshop_week_s1_p1' => 'A workshop week on the Six-Month Course follows the day-course hours, Monday to Thursday, 8:30 to 16:30. Monday is demonstration and drawing. The joint, the drawing and the standard for the week are set before the booths are used in earnest. Tuesday and Wednesday are spent in the booth. Students work the process of that month on their own piece, with two instructors on the floor for the cohort of 12. Thursday morning is cut, bend and review. Thursday afternoon is clean down and the next week\'s drawing. The order does not swap. Drawing comes before booth time, and review comes before the next drawing is issued. Friday is not a teaching day.',
	'workshop_week_s2_h' => 'The review rack',
	'workshop_week_s2_p1' => 'Each week ends with one marked piece on the review rack. The rack holds the finished practice pieces where the cohort can look at them again, not only the person who made the piece. A T-joint from the MIG month, a coupon opened on Thursday morning, or a later fabrication all pass through the same habit: one piece, marked, put up at the end of the week. The mark is the college\'s reading of that week\'s work. It is not a qualification and it is not a job offer. Thursday afternoon\'s clean down clears the bench so the next drawing starts on a tidy booth. The following Monday\'s demonstration takes up from what the rack showed.',
	'workshop_week_s3_h' => 'What the week is inside',
	'workshop_week_s3_p1' => 'The week is the same shape in every month of the 26. What changes is the work inside it. Early weeks produce a squared, cut and filed steel block, then flat and horizontal fillets, then a vertical-up fillet. Later weeks move to a stainless butt joint, a welded steel gate frame, and at the end a test plate set. Seven taught hours a day, four days a week, across 26 weeks, is how the course reaches 728 hours. Nothing about the week depends on a student having welded before. Experience is not required when someone applies. The part-time job many students keep sits on Friday, outside these hours, and the college does not schedule teaching against it.',
);
